Absolutely! Here's a C# function that calculates the average daily weight gain (ADG) using dates instead of the number of days between:
```csharp
using System;

namespace ADGCalculator
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            DateTime initialDate = new DateTime(2023, 1, 1);
            DateTime finalDate = new DateTime(2023, 1, 31);
            double initialWeight = 300;
            double finalWeight = 400;

            double adg = CalculateADG(initialDate, finalDate, initialWeight, finalWeight);
            Console.WriteLine($"The average daily weight gain is: {adg} lbs/day");
        }

        static double CalculateADG(DateTime initialDate, DateTime finalDate, double initialWeight, double finalWeight)
        {
            if (initialDate >= finalDate)
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("The initial date must be earlier than the final date.");
            }

            TimeSpan timeSpan = finalDate - initialDate;
            int daysBetween = timeSpan.Days;

            double weightDifference = finalWeight - initialWeight;
            double adg = weightDifference / daysBetween;

            return adg;
        }
    }
}
```
This function, `CalculateADG`, takes the initial and final dates, along with the initial and final weights, as parameters and returns the calculated ADG. It also checks if the initial date is earlier than the final date to avoid negative or zero days between.

You can customize the `initialDate`, `finalDate`, `initialWeight`, and `finalWeight` variables in the `Main` method to calculate the ADG for different scenarios.

